BossLore (
    boss_id: "eclipse_walker",
    canonical_name: "The Eclipse Walker",
    world: "mimas",
    biome: "microgravity_caverns",
    arena_rule: "TwoWayDoor",
    entries: [
        BossLoreEntry (
            kind: "origin_myth",
            title: "What the Walker Used to Be",
            body: "The Eclipse Walker is, by every Concordat record, a Mimas methane-breather of unusually advanced age — approximately three thousand four hundred and sixty Earth-years old by the methane-breather elders' own count, which makes it the oldest individual the Concordat has documented in any species. The Walker is not, in the methane-breathers' framing, hostile to humans. The Walker is, by methane-breather framing, lonely. The Walker has, in its lifetime, lost approximately seven hundred and forty-one members of its extended family group, and is, by the elders' own assessment, no longer capable of holding the resonance of the family. The Walker has retreated, in the last two hundred Earth-years, to the deep microgravity caverns on the trailing hemisphere, where it does not, by methane-breather agreement, receive visitors. Visitors arrive anyway. The Walker, by its own reckoning, has become accustomed to them."
        ),
        BossLoreEntry (
            kind: "war_history",
            title: "The Three Visits",
            body: "The Eclipse Walker has, in Concordat records, been formally engaged on three occasions. The first was by a Coalition reconnaissance team in 2217 that mapped the cavern system, encountered the Walker, and retreated without firing a shot; the team's after-action report describes the Walker as 'an organism of substantial dignity and unclear posture toward the visiting force'. The second was a Collegium expedition in 2228 that attempted to obtain a tissue sample; the expedition's three personnel were not killed but were 'reoriented' — the Collegium's after-action describes the personnel as having returned with their memories of the cavern intact but their understanding of the events shifted in ways the Collegium has not been able to reproduce in any controlled test. The third engagement, in 2235, was a Starlight-Collegium joint expedition that committed to a full encounter and produced both the encounter framework that subsequent expeditions follow and the only confirmed sample of Eclipse Walker biological material currently in any Concordat archive. The expedition lost two personnel; the sample, by the Collegium's protocols, has been preserved without analysis at the methane-breather attache's request."
        ),
        BossLoreEntry (
            kind: "defeat_consequences",
            title: "What the Walker's Grief Costs",
            body: "Engaging the Eclipse Walker and reducing it below the dormancy threshold produces the Micro-Gravity Boots recipe, the Cyborg Walker's Claw artifact, and three lore unlocks. The mechanical cost is the heaviest single jet-pack-fuel burn in any current PvE engagement; the player must, in this encounter, sustain mobility across three phases in active microgravity, and the encounter is by every Concordat guide unwinnable without a jet pack at full charge. The narrative cost is, by methane-breather framing, the most expensive of the five endgame bosses. Driving the Walker into dormancy produces, in the methane-breathers' oldest grief protocol, an obligation on the entire methane-breather community to sing for fourteen Earth-days. The Concordat has, in writing, apologized after every successful dispersal of the Walker. The methane-breathers have, after the third successful dispersal, asked the Concordat to stop sending expeditions. The Concordat has not, in any current policy, complied."
        ),
        BossLoreEntry (
            kind: "phase_descriptions",
            title: "The Three Movements",
            body: "Phase One: the Walker manifests as a large, agile cyborg-form organism that moves across the cavern's walls and ceiling at unusual speed for its size. The Walker in this phase is, by tactical analysis, primarily evasive rather than aggressive; the player must, in this phase, close distance through the microgravity cavern terrain. The phase ends when the player makes sustained contact. Phase Two: the cavern's gravity inverts. The microgravity field shifts in three distinct stages over approximately ninety seconds. The Walker uses the shifting gravity to reposition; the player must, in this phase, maintain orientation while the floor and ceiling exchange positions multiple times. The phase ends when the gravity stabilizes. Phase Three: the Walker enters sustained combat at close range. The fighting style is, by Concordat analysis, derived from the methane-breather hunting traditions that the Walker learned in its youth, with adaptations for the cyborg implants the Walker has accumulated over its lifetime. The phase ends, and the encounter concludes in dormancy, when the Walker's resonance lowers to the methane-breather grief threshold. The Walker does not, in this phase, die. The Walker withdraws further into the deep caverns."
        ),
        BossLoreEntry (
            kind: "dropped_lore_codex",
            title: "Methane-Breather Elder Statement, 2235",
            body: "Translated from the methane-breather dialect by the Collegium translation team, recorded after the third engagement. Speaker is identified only as 'an elder of the Patient Ones' by methane-breather convention. The translation is approximate; the methane-breather elder's original statement was approximately four hundred and seventy syllables long and took approximately fourteen Earth-minutes to deliver. 'You ask why we sing for the Walker. We sing because the Walker no longer sings, and the song does not, in our convention, permit a silence in the place where a singer used to be. The Walker was, at its peak, one of the most resonant carriers of the family song that the patient ones produced in our recorded history. The Walker has lost the family. The family is, in our convention, not yours to give back. You cannot, by any combat or by any sample, repair what your expeditions are doing to the Walker. You can only stop. We have, on three occasions, asked you to stop. We are, in this statement, asking again. We are not, in this statement, angry. We are, in this statement, mourning. The mourning is, in our convention, the most patient form of disagreement that the patient ones produce. We will, in our convention, continue mourning. We hope, in this statement, that the mourning eventually persuades. If it does not, we will continue. The song does not, in our convention, give up on any singer.'"
        ),
    ],
)
